        {
            "id": "the_fair_price",
            "zone": "wendford",
            "when": "arrival",
            "target": "caravan",
            "owner_pick": "talk",
            "once": "contract",
            "text": "A crowd stands in front of Fenn's wagon on the Wendford green. A woman with a basket says what they all think: salt is dear this year, dearer than anyone remembers, and they want Fenn's salt here, at their price, before it goes up the hill.",
            "note": "Price is the signal of change, and its cause is off the map: nobody on the green knows why salt is dear, only that it is. Market against custom: Fenn has sold at his price for years, the valley thinks a fair price is the one it always paid, and neither is right. It is Fenn's salt, so Fenn decides with the Blades' advice; he says \"Decide it between you\" and means it. Nell Hale, the reeve's niece, about eight, sits on the rim of the well with a slate and does the crowd's sums for them, out loud and wrong on purpose. Nobody is hurt: making way means pushing through people, never the wagon over them. If the Blades paid the company's toll at the bridge (paid_the_company), they carry its paper: two silver a head on salt that used to cross for nothing is part of why it is dear, and the crowd can see the seal.",
